Thank you so much for taking the time to sit down with me. Lovely to meet you for the 1st time. Likewise. I know you're gonna be taking the seat of, or you're looking to replace Senator Cinema. Yes. Going in. I mean, we've heard Republicans talk about her as she's announced that she is not looking for a second term, that she has been a very effective senator in her first term, just in terms of the amount of legislation she's worked on. When it comes to you taking on that baton, how do you feel about your approach when it comes to becoming a leader and not a bench warmer in the Senate? I think that's exactly why I'm in this, because I look sometimes at DC, and I think, Wow, we send these people there. We vote for them. They get there and they don't even remember what they campaigned on. when they were back at home. And I want to go there and remember exactly what I campaign done, which is turning this nightmare around, securing the border, making our streets safe, and making our economy strong. And so I want to go there and be effective. I'm not going to DC because I want a career in politics. I don't. I frankly want to go in, get the work done, and come back home to my life here in Arizona. But we have to go there and get the work done. And I've seen too many people who go there and just want to be politicians. We need to get those kind of people out. That's who I'm running again, somebody who just wants to be a lifelong, lifer politician, and get people in there who are moms who've led a real normal life, who want to go in and get some work done, and then come back home. I don't want people to go to D.C. forever. That's not how our founding fathers set this thing up. They never envisioned anybody would want to go to D.C. and stayed there forever. So I do plan to get things down. I plan to be a mover and shaker, and do that for the people of Arizona. When it comes to being in D.C. We know that compromise is part of the game, and unfortunately, a lot of the priorities that you have may not all make it to the finish line. So if you have to choose a top three, what would you say your top three priorities are? Number one, number one, and number one, is going to be border, because national security and safety in our communities, and the economy. Right now, everywhere I go, talking to people in Arizona, they say, I can't afford the groceries when I go to the grocery store. And guess what? I don't feel safe walking across the grocery store parking lot anymore. So we have to tackle both of those. The border has many tentacles. The border, obviously, we have an invasion at the border. Millions of people have poured across. Our taxpayer dollars are going to house them, feed them, and set them up. Meanwhile we have Americans struggling. But the border also means being wide open. Drugs are pouring in, getting into the hands of our young people. I've met so many moms and dads who've lost a son, 19 year old son, 20 year old daughter, and they don't get those babies back. They don't get their kids back. We got to secure that border. This is not because we're uncompassionate. We're actually the most compassionate people on the planet here in America. But this is not compassionate to our own people, leaving that border open. And it's affecting our economy as well. So those two are really big. I'm so concerned right now for a couple of groups in our population, one, the young people. My daughter is almost 21. She'll be 21 in a couple days. My son is 19 and they say, Mom, we're doing everything right, but I don't know that we'll ever be able to afford a home. Heck, maybe not even build afford a car anymore. How do we live the American dream? Concerned about that group, even if they do everything right, it's gonna be hard to get ahead and have that security in this economy. And I'm really concerned about people who are nearing retirement or, frankly, in retirement, who did everything right their whole life. They paid into their taxes, they paid into Social Security, and now they're looking at that little nest egg they put together, and they say, It's not gonna get me through because they of Joe Biden's inflation. And Reuben Gallego's inflation, frankly, the Biden inflation, Biden economics is not working. And so, I met a woman who was almost 90, not too long ago, she was not almost 80, rather. She was about to turn 80 when she looked phenomenal, by the way. And she said, Carrie, I don't think I'm gonna make it through the rest of my retirement. and be able to survive. And I don't think anybody wants to hire an 80 year old. And I was just blown away by that. First of all, you shouldn't have to be worried at 80 to go back into the workforce. This Biden economy, the Democratic economy is not working. We need to get back to economy that does work for all people. And whether you loved him or hated him, President Trump got it, that Trump economy made sure that everybody in the population, no matter what their skin color, what their background, what their political affiliation, was doing better. So let's get back to that, something that works. Those are my priorities. And then I know that there's many more beyond that. I've seen your website and everything, but the top three there, yeah. You talked about economy. I know that we just saw that the Congress has, once again, kicked the can down the road in terms of its spending. So that's the thing that, you know, we hear in D.C. You know, we don't want to do this, but every year they do approve those changes. If you were in charge of it, and you're part of that conversation, What would you be in support of reducing in the budget, or in our government spending, and maybe what would you advocate for? Well, I mean, we're at, like, 34 trillion, more than that now, in deficit. Can you imagine a household living? First of all, if you lived the way our government lives, your all of your credit cards, it'd be shut off, you'd be kicked out of your house, and you'd be on the street. I mean, this, you can't survive with this kind of spending. We can't survive as a country with this kind of spending. And so, but we can't just cut our way out of it. I mean, there's such a deficit cutting our way out of it. It's not gonna work. We need to start growing our way out of it. And unfortunately, Joe Biden made it worse when he came in on day one, it ended the Keystone Excel pipeline. That caused so many high paying jobs to go away. The Democrats had been for shipping, manufacturing overseas. The Democrats, and frankly, a few Republicans have been for exporting all of our great American companies over to where they can operate on pennies on the dollar, and that means when they do that, our good, high paying jobs go away as well. High paying good jobs are needed for the American people, and we can bring those back when we reshore American companies. I think Arizona's uniquely positioned to take some of that reassuring of companies. companies do want to come back. We did not punish them when they want to come back, and we need to realize that when those companies come back, they're going to be hiring tens of thousands, hundreds of thousands of Americans who need jobs, not just service industry jobs, where they're making minimum wage, but jobs where they're making enough money to feed a family to put a down payment on a house. And then the benefit for the government is, more tax revenues are coming in. So it's going to have to be, we cut, but we also grow our way out of this mess. Talking about that investment in our economy here in Arizona. We saw President Biden here last week. He's talking about intel and the Chips Act. Is that something that you would have supported had you been in Congress? I do support the Chips Act. I mean, we're gonna be the hub for chips making. You know, by processing. The problem is, in typical Biden fashion, he screws things up. Hate to say it, but he's messing things up. And when they tied all that DEI stuff, diversity, equity, and inclusion. You know, somebody said the other day, it means didn't earn it. When they tie all of that to it, and they force a chips maker to hire people based on just things that aren't about merit. They don't want to do it. All these companies that want to take that money, and relocate, and start up a business here, and grow in the chips manufacturing world, are saying, Hmm, we look at all of these regulations. We're going to pass on that. We've got to pull that away. We've got to pull all that DEI stuff away and just say, Are we going to be a hub for chip processing or not? Or are we going to be a hub for wokeness, and wokeness is going to destroy our country? I know you've mentioned inflation. It's on the minds of anyone who's buying groceries, frankly. You know, the prices, or I think everyone's feeling those in their pockets books right now. But how much do you think is it when it comes to inflation versus corporate greed? 'Cause just to kind of take you through some of the stats we have here right now. In Cargirl, we saw corporate profits were rising to 165 billion in 2022, up from the previous year, Tyson Foods, double profits in 2022. The Walmart family had their wealth increased by 8.8 billion between 20 and 2022. So I think those are times where we really felt that in our pockets, and seeing those companies just have record profits and shrink inflation at the same time, I mean, how much can be done, do you think, about that? Well, I think for a long time, you know, all those companies that you mentioned, this multinational corporations, they've got lots of lobbyists in D.C. and these lobbyists get there, and they bribe and cajole all these members of Congress to vote, where they're only supporting that segment of the business community. I care about midsize businesses, small businesses, businesses that, you know, Americans have set up. And so we need to make sure we're looking at that. And one of the concerns is, coming into January of 25, right around inauguration day, is many of the Trump tax cuts for small businesses. If you got an LLC behind your business, many Arizonans have that. Those tax cuts are gonna jump back up. They're going to go away. And I don't know how many people who run a business, whether it be a one man business with just, you know, you're running it alone, or you've got 100 people. A smaller business, how many of them can afford a 25, 30% tax rise, tax increase right away. That's what we're going to get if Ruben Gallego becomes our senator. He will not vote to keep those tax cuts that Trump brought us. So we've got to make sure if you're a business person, and you're wor let me know. Can you afford a 30% tax increase? I don't know many people who can right now. And so we got to work on that. Yes, there is a lot of corporate greed. These, you know, multibillionaires, and they, um, raise the prices. With inflation, the problem is, when inflation goes down, the cost never seem to go down. Have you noticed that? Oh, wow, this the price of this went up during inflation. Okay, inflation came, the rate came back down, but the price of that is still really high, and we're all feeling the effects of that. And so we need to start looking at small businesses, and are we helping them? Because that's how we grow our economy through small businesses, not just a few multinational corporations. Would you anticipate seeing during this time where you're in a period of raising money, ever taking donations from those larger businesses like that? You mean, like, pack donations? I mean, you know, we are I'm so blessed. I have, you know, the media doesn't necessarily give me a fair shake, but the people get it. They get that for them. I am for the people. I'm not in this for personal gain at all. I'm not gaining anything. I'm in this for the people. And we have the second highest number of small donors on our campaign. I think in our governor's history, we had, like, 215 or 250,000 individual donors. I can't even keep track of where they were. Some of them were moms who would send me $20 and say, We can't afford more than this. We're following you. Thank you for fighting for us. They'd send me little pictures that their five and seven year old drew for me. Warm my heart, 'cause my kids, doesn't seem like it was too long ago, they were five and seven. And so I have a lot of that, but I might also have max donors who, maybe they do run a business or work for a corporation, and they care also about the economy. I'm not gonna say I'm not gonna take someone's donation, if they care about, and they see, the campaign we're running, and they say, wow, she's really working to put America first. She's working to put Arizona first. If you want to make a donation to my campaign, you go to kerrylake.com, slash donate and make that donation. I'm happy to accept a donation if somebody is watching our campaign, saying, We like the direction. She's leading her campaign and that she'd like to lead this country. I don't wanna ignore your second point that you're passionate about, which is the border. I know it's on the minds of many people here in Arizona, and across... Let me say one more thing. Second, in small donors, second only, to President Donald J. Trump. So there's some movement going on here in Arizona, and the media might want to deny it, but there's a movement going on with people who've never been involved in politics. that most of the letters we get. They're not just a check. Here is a donation. It is a letter. You inspire me. Your campaign inspires me. I lost, you know, my child to fentanyl poisoning, or I lost my job, because my company had to shut down, and they stopped hiring people. People are suffering right now, and they see our campaign. They see me as somebody who wants to go to D.C. and not be beholden to lobbyists. not be bribable. They've already tried to bribe me, and I told them to pound sand. I'm gonna go and work for the people. And that's why there's so much pushback on people like me and President Trump. Speaking of the border, just to get back to that. I know that that's important to you and many people here in Arizona. Aspects of your 10 point border plan. Reading through that, being familiar with the bipartisan border plan, I do see similarities between some of them, some of the things that you're looking for and some of the things that were proposed in that. So, you've been to the border, you've talked about how bad it is. You've seen how bad it is down there. Why support putting the people down there through another year of what they're experiencing when it comes to the border. So why didn't I support that? Yeah. Well, first of all, another year is gonna be painful. I didn't want to put them through a lifetime of that what they're seeing now, being permanent. And that piece of legislation was so disastrous, we would have seen a permanent situation, permanent invasion. That was a, I forget, it was $130 billion. It was more than that. 130, I think. I might be off. Right around there, a billion dollar border plan, that had zero dollars to build the wall. The cheapest way to secure that border is to finish the wall at the border. There was a big, huge goose egg, zero dollars. for that in that border bill. Meanwhile, there's 95 billion to be sent overseas to kill people. I don't believe the people of Arizona are for that. I want true border security. And we could have had it much smaller. First thing they should have done is said, let's set aside between $5,000 and $15 billion. You can look at the estimates. I know that's a wide range, but from what I'm hearing, it'd be 5 to 15, probably closer to 15 billion to build that wall. That should be just a super simple one or two page piece of legislation. Here's the money, and we want to expedite the building of the border wall. That is what our lawmakers in D.C. should be doing. Not some big piece of legislation that's 500,000 pages of a bunch of nonsense, where what they want to do, and what my opponent, Reuben Gallego, wants to do, is he wants to actually send hundreds of billions to the border communities, and to the border, and to these NGOs, NGOs, who are part of the problem in bringing people here to continue to process them faster. I have news for Ruben. The people of Arizona and the people of America don't want to keep processing people pouring across illegally faster. They actually want to process them and send them back to their homeland. And that's what we're going to do. The states have to start stepping up right now until Joe Biden's out of office when President Trump gets back in office. When I get to the U.S. Senate, we are going to quickly stop this invasion at our border. And in order to save our homeland, we're going to have to send the people who've invaded our country back to their homeland. And the largest repatriation program this country's ever seen. And we're gonna make those countries pick up the tab. I'm sure you've spoken, though, with people at the border about that plan, and I've done so over the last couple of months, and the overall message I was hearing, was, this may not be a perfect plan, but we just, we need something. I mean... But it's gonna hurt them in the long run. It's gonna mean that we're taking five, we're gonna take millions and millions of people illegally continuing forever. And I don't think they realize how complex and how terrible this piece of legislation is. And frankly, the media doesn't go down and explain it. So when you ask, do you support a border bill that would have helped you? Well, naturally, someone's gonna go, oh, if it helped, yeah. But what you're not explaining is how it's going to cement and codify, into law, forever, the invasion of our country by millions of people every year. Oh, I wrote my statement on that. I wasn't speaking with just everyday voters, who I do believe are very informed, of course, but these are border mayors, who are very clear about what's in 